      "claim": "DeepAgents is a LangChain harness built on LangGraph, providing planning, sub-agents, filesystem abstraction, and human-in-the-loop controls; for RefractRouter v0.1 it serves as the fixed-DAG execution harness with autonomous planning and dynamic sub-agent generation disabled."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "source_002",
      "title": "LangGraph",
      "content_hash": "97960ed618df652ae16f900a1ea677e257684118f5eab68686b2f8713ae74960",
      "claim": "LangGraph is a graph state machine framework supporting explicit nodes, edges, durable state, and check-pointing, and RefractRouter uses it as the lower-level execution substrate for a fixed task DAG."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "source_003",
      "title": "Pydantic AI",
      "content_hash": "7edd7c4a831f8cb06e0c9fc8b17911818ad63ff686b7d7245b176c46a3cea42e",
      "claim": "Pydantic AI provides typed tool definitions and structured model outputs, useful when strict schemas are required, but RefractRouter v0.1 does not need it as a core dependency unless structured-output parsing becomes a bottleneck."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "source_004",
      "title": "DeepSeek Harness",
      "content_hash": "b06d562fa3e636b8ffb02c1578e300033ae7f23672b90445a9489bbe65b57331",
      "claim": "DSH (DeepSeek Harness) is an agent harness where the agent loop itself is a plugin, and for RefractRouter v0.1 it is reserved for the outer validation environment, not the core routing loop."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "source_005",
      "title": "LLM routing research gap",
      "content_hash": "418b6cda722a8338f10fa145fb4f4f5c3f188ff413651e4d73aa2949b4e07460",
      "claim": "Existing LLM routing work mostly selects one model for an entire request, whereas RefractRouter studies whether selecting a model separately for each node in a task DAG can improve the quality-cost-latency Pareto frontier."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "source_006",
      "title": "Task decomposition boundary",
      "content_hash": "96a837f9788f5d0c6d9ab2cc9633775c42dc5569afc70b1782dc1d6d7a198504",
      "claim": "Static task decomposition belongs to the application layer, while the router receives the DAG as input and focuses on per-node model selection; dynamic re-splitting is deferred until node-level routing is validated."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "source_007",
      "title": "Benchmark design",
      "content_hash": "03e9443028f0065ae41218c422efdcebe73225465468098c9ba49f03596be4d0",
      "claim": "A useful routing benchmark needs fixed task DAGs, multiple candidate models, per-node and per-task scoring, cost accounting, and critical-path latency, and the first benchmark should stay small enough to reproduce."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "source_008",
      "title": "Scoring rubric",
